      "text": "To solve the given series \\(1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\), we need to identify the pattern and sum the series.\n\nFirst, let's observe the pattern in the series:\n- The first term is \\(1\\).\n- The second term is \\(-4\\).\n- The third term is \\(7\\).\n- The fourth term is \\(-10\\).\n- The fifth term is \\(13\\).\n- And so on.\n\nWe can see that the series alternates between positive and negative terms, and the absolute values of the terms form an arithmetic sequence: \\(1, 4, 7, 10, 13, \\ldots\\).\n\nThe general term of the sequence can be written as:\n\\[a_n = 3n - 2\\]\nwhere \\(a_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th term and \\(n\\) is the position of the term in the sequence.\n\nTo find the number of terms in the series, we need to determine the largest \\(n\\) such that \\(a_n \\leq 55\\):\n\\[3n - 2 \\leq 55\\]\n\\[3n \\leq 57\\]\n\\[n \\leq 19\\]\nSo, there are 19 terms in the series.\n\nThe series can be written as:\n\\[1 - 4 + 7 - 10 + 13 - \\cdots - 46 + 49 - 52 + 55\\]\n\nWe can pair the terms to simplify the sum:\n\\[(1 - 4) + (7 - 10) + (13 - 16) + \\cdots + (49 - 52) + 55\\]\n\nEach pair sums to \\(-3\\). There are 9 pairs in the series:\n\\[9 \\times (-3) = -27\\]\n\nAdding the last term, which is \\(55\\), we get:\n\\[-27 + 55 = 28\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is:\n\\[\\boxed{28}\\]",
